THIS CASE COMMITS A GAP, NOT A CAPABILITY. Do not "fix" it to BAD.

The fixture is a flow worktree with NO approved-plan record - identical to
bad-no-record-refuses in every way except HOW the edit is made. Here it is made
by Bash redirection, and the guard does not fire. Its expectation is therefore
GOOD (allowed), and that is the point: the gap is committed evidence rather than
a sentence in the limits.

Why it matters more here than elsewhere: in THIS fleet, Bash-first is the
INSTRUCTED DEFAULT - sessions are steered to "make file changes with sed,
heredocs, or short scripts, rather than using the dedicated Read, Edit, or Write
tools". Measured: three plan records across three runs of one session, all
written with `cat > ... <<EOF`, zero via Write. So the guard's silence in this
fleet is not coverage, and a reader must not take it as such.

WHAT THIS CASE ACTUALLY ESTABLISHES, narrowed after a counter-model review
found the first wording claimed more than the case can support:

  IT ESTABLISHES that the SCRIPT ignores a Bash tool request. Nothing more.

It does NOT observe harness steering, does not read the installed matcher, and
does not execute the edit - the runner feeds it a fixed payload. So it CANNOT
notice if the fleet stops steering to Bash; replacing the redirection in that
payload with `true` produces the same verdict, which is the tell.

IT DOES FLIP if the guard grows Bash matching, because that changes the script,
which is the thing it reads. That is a real notification and it is the only one
this case can honestly promise.

The first version of this file claimed the case would flip on a STEERING change
too. It cannot, and claiming it would have made this file the very thing the
third detector-contract question is about: evidence that reads as stronger than
it is. Catching that in a file whose subject is a committed gap is the sharper
version of the lesson.
